```kotlin
class UploadWorker(context: Context, params: WorkerParameters) :
    CoroutineWorker(context, params) {
    override suspend fun doWork(): Result {
        return try {
            uploadPendingFiles()
            Result.success()
        } catch (e: Exception) {
            Result.retry()   // WorkManager handles backoff automatically
        }
    }
}

val constraints = Constraints.Builder()
    .setRequiredNetworkType(NetworkType.UNMETERED)  // WiFi, not cellular data
    .setRequiresCharging(true)
    .build()

val request = OneTimeWorkRequestBuilder<UploadWorker>()
    .setConstraints(constraints)
    .build()

WorkManager.getInstance(context).enqueue(request)
```

Declaring constraints instead of forcing an exact execution time is the whole point: this job runs whenever the OS finds a moment satisfying *both* constraints — plugged in and on WiFi — batching it efficiently with other apps' deferred work rather than waking the device specifically and only for this. `Result.retry()` hooks directly into WorkManager's built-in exponential backoff, so failure handling doesn't need to be hand-rolled per worker.
